Response: # Temperature Data Acquisition System (TDAS) Design Specification

## 1. System Architecture Description

The system is designed as a deterministic, non-blocking data loop running on an MCU (e.g., ARM Cortex-M, AVR, or ESP32). It utilizes an analog-to-digital converter (ADC) to sample a voltage-output temperature sensor. The core logic separates data acquisition from processing to ensure timing jitter is minimized.

**ASCII Block Diagram:**

```text
+----------------+       +----------------+       +-----------------------+
|   Sensor       |       |   MCU Core    |       |   Reporting Layer     |
| (Analog Voltage|----->|   (ADC + Logic)|----->|   (UART / Log / LED)  |
|   0-5V)        |       |               |       |                       |
+----------------+       |  +---------+  |       +-----------------------+
                         | |  ADC    |  |
                         | +---------+  |
                         | |  Timer  |  |
                         | +---------+  |
                         | |  State  |  |
                         | |  Machine|  |
                         | +---------+  |
                         +------+-------+
                                |
                          (Interrupts/
                           GPIO Status)
```

**Key Design Decisions:**
*   **Interface:** Analog Voltage (0–5V) mapped via ADC. This requires explicit conversion logic in firmware, ensuring the "raw to engineering unit" requirement is met programmatically.
*   **Timing:** Non-blocking timer logic (`millis()`-style) prevents the main control loop from freezing during data processing.
*   **Filtering:** A combination of Moving Average (for noise) and Consecutive Counting (for glitch immunity) is used.

